Mixed Connective Tissue Disease (Mild Arthralgia)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Puffy fingers, mild joint aches, low-grade fatigue without severe organ involvement

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-033
Reason for Visit: Acute severe fatigue, diffuse myalgia, and marked finger swelling requiring exclusion of an acute systemic inflammatory crisis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate the rapid onset of diffuse bodily pain, severe fatigue, and sudden hand swelling. Immediate clinical review was necessary to differentiate a benign autoimmune flare from acute viral myositis, new-onset acute lupus crisis, or severe systemic infection prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I woke up feeling like I've been hit by a truck, my whole body aches, and my fingers are so puffed up they look like sausages.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a history of presence of anti-U1 RNP with mild clinical signs. Today, the patient experienced an acute escalation of symptoms including profound, low-grade fatigue, diffuse myalgia, and visually distressing bilateral hand swelling. The severe lethargy has left the patient largely bedbound since the afternoon, triggering severe anxiety regarding organ failure or an impending critical systemic collapse. Denies shortness of breath, chest pain, fever, or new rashes.
Functional Status: Markedly reduced, mobilizing only for essential needs due to fatigue.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone, poor oral intake today due to lethargy.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/75 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.2 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, appearing acutely fatigued and moving stiffly. No acute respiratory distress.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal exam reveals diffuse tenderness to deep palpation over large muscle groups globally. Bilateral hands show diffuse, non-pitting edema of the digits ("puffy fingers") but lack acute synovitis, erythema, or severe localized joint tenderness. Cardiovascular and respiratory exams are strictly normal. Abdomen is soft. No active malar rash. History and findings are consistent with puffy fingers, mild joint aches, low-grade fatigue without severe organ involvement.
Environmental Observations: House is cold; advised patient to use blankets and heating.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient occasionally uses NSAIDs but has not taken any today due to nausea.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute systemic flare of Mixed Connective Tissue Disease (Mild Arthralgia) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Viral Illness (e.g., Influenza/COVID) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete lack of upper respiratory symptoms, cough, or true high fever, and the specific finding of diffuse digital swelling.

Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) Crisis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of pleuritis/pericarditis, normal hemodynamics, and no new cutaneous manifestations (rash/ulcers).

Systemic Sclerosis Crisis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal blood pressure (no renal crisis), normal oxygenation, and no acute digital ischemia or necrosis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (living alone with severe fatigue)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, profound fatigue and diffuse swelling, raising clinical concern for a severe systemic inflammatory event or covert infection. Triage appropriately escalated the call to perform a comprehensive physical examination. Bedside evaluation firmly established normal hemodynamics, clear lung fields, and isolated the physical signs to a diffuse, non-critical connective tissue flare. This assessment definitively ruled out life-threatening organ involvement (renal, pulmonary, cardiac) and safely averted an inappropriate late-night hospital admission for a patient who primarily required targeted analgesia, rest, and reassurance, satisfying the criteria for urgent after-hours intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral simple analgesia and advised on hydration. Instructed patient to rest and keep hands warm.
Positive Behavioral Change: Take simple paracetamol with a full glass of warm water and immediately wrap yourself in a heated blanket. Raising your core body temperature and ensuring adequate hydration will specifically reduce the muscle aching and improve blood flow, clearing the fluid from your swollen fingers by morning.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48-72 hours to assess flare resolution and discuss potential immunomodulatory therapy (e.g., hydroxychloroquine).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you develop sharp pain in your chest when breathing in, severe shortness of breath, a high fever, or if you become confused.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive regarding the nature of the flare; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: This severe fatigue and generalized swelling is your immune system misfiring. It causes a profound "flu-like" exhaustion without an actual infection. It will stabilize with rest and medication.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical attention if your fingers turn suddenly stark white or blue and become agonizingly painful, indicating a severe blood vessel spasm.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your body is experiencing a generalized inflammatory reaction, primarily affecting the soft tissues and causing fluid retention in the hands. There is no damage to your internal organs tonight.
Actionable Steps: Rest completely tomorrow. Drink plenty of warm fluids. You must see your GP this week to get a long-term plan to prevent these sudden "crashes."

